<h2>Automated reminders that actually get read</h2>

<p>Setting up online booking is the first step. The second is making sure people actually show up. That is where automated reminders come in.</p>

<p>The ideal reminder sequence for a salon or spa looks like this:</p>

<p><strong>Booking confirmation (immediate).</strong> The moment a client books, they receive an email confirming the date, time, service, and stylist. This sets the tone and makes the appointment feel real. Include a link to reschedule or cancel, because making it easy to cancel actually reduces no-shows. People who would have ghosted you will cancel instead, freeing up the slot for someone else.</p>

<p><strong>Email reminder (24 hours before).</strong> A day before the appointment, send a reminder with all the details. For <a href="/blog/email-marketing-small-business">email to be effective</a>, keep it short and scannable. Include any preparation instructions, such as arriving with clean hair for a colour service or avoiding moisturiser before a wax.</p>

<p><strong>SMS reminder (2 hours before).</strong> This is the one that makes the real difference. <a href="/blog/sms-marketing-small-business">Text messages</a> have open rates above 95%, and most are read within three minutes. A brief, friendly message two hours before the appointment catches the people who missed the email or simply forgot. Something like: "Hi Sarah, just a reminder about your cut and colour with Emma at 2pm today. See you soon!"</p>

<p>The beauty of <strong>spa automation</strong> is that this entire sequence runs without anyone on your team lifting a finger. You set it up once, and every single booking gets the same professional, consistent follow-up. No sticky notes, no mental checklists, no "did we remind Mrs Johnson about her 3pm?"</p>

<blockquote><p>SMS reminders have a 95%+ open rate and are typically read within three minutes. For last-minute nudges, nothing else comes close.</p></blockquote>

<h2>Rebooking campaigns for lapsed clients</h2>

<p>In the beauty industry, client retention is everything. Acquiring a new client costs five to seven times more than keeping an existing one. Yet most salons have no system for reaching out to clients who have quietly stopped coming in.</p>

<p>Think about your own client list. How many people came in regularly for six months, then just disappeared? A gentle, automated nudge is often all it takes to bring them back.</p>

<h3>How automated rebooking works</h3>

<p>A smart <strong>beauty business CRM</strong> tracks when each client last visited. When someone has not booked in a set period, say six or eight weeks for a regular haircut client or three months for a facial client, the system automatically sends a personalised message.</p>

<p>"Hi Sarah, it has been a while since your last visit. We would love to see you again. Book your next appointment here: [link]."</p>

<p>You can layer in offers for lapsed clients if you want to. A small incentive, like 10% off their next visit or a complimentary treatment add-on, can be the nudge that gets someone back in the chair. But often, just the reminder itself is enough. People appreciate being thought of.</p>

<p>This is the same principle behind a good <a href="/blog/customer-follow-up-system">customer follow-up system</a>. The difference is that in a salon context, you can be very specific about timing, because you know how often each client typically visits. For clients who have not visited in six months or longer, a "we miss you" campaign with a stronger offer can reactivate people you thought were gone for good.</p>

<h2>Client preferences tracking: remembering what your clients love</h2>

<p>Great salon service has always been personal. Your best clients expect you to remember their usual colour formula, their preferred stylist, and that they always want a coffee when they arrive. Traditionally, this knowledge lived in the stylist's head or on scraps of paper tucked into a card file.</p>

<p>A proper beauty business CRM centralises all of this. Every client has a profile that tracks:</p>

<p><strong>Service history.</strong> What they have had done, when, and with whom. This is invaluable when a client calls and says "I want the same thing I had last time" and you are not sure which stylist they saw or which shade they used.</p>

<p><strong>Product preferences.</strong> What products they buy, what they have tried, and what they liked. This lets you make relevant recommendations at checkout and send targeted promotions for products they actually care about.</p>

<p><strong>Stylist preferences.</strong> Some clients are loyal to one team member. Others are happy with anyone. Tracking this means you never accidentally book someone with a stylist they do not prefer.</p>

<p><strong>Notes and special requirements.</strong> Allergies, sensitivities, mobility needs, or just the fact that they always run ten minutes late. Having this information visible to your whole team means every client gets a consistent, personal experience, regardless of who is behind the chair.</p>

<p>When your team has this information at their fingertips, every interaction feels seamless. The client does not have to repeat themselves. The stylist is prepared. And your product recommendations are relevant rather than random.</p>

